        "The effect of unconditional income on work is the most contested question in the UBI debate. Standard labour supply theory predicts that additional unearned income reduces hours worked through an income effect, while removing the withdrawal of benefits when earnings rise should increase work by lowering effective marginal tax rates [3][25]. The 1970s negative income tax experiments found reductions in annual hours of about 5 to 7 percent for primary earners and larger reductions for second earners and youth [11][12], while evidence from lottery winners suggests modest reductions in earnings that are proportional to the size of the windfall [15][16]. In low- and middle-income countries, reviews of cash transfers find little evidence of reduced work [9][17][18], and the permanent and universal Alaska dividend has had no effect on employment while raising part-time work [10].",
