        "If the labour supply effects of the pilots are modest, their effects on wellbeing are consistently positive and, relative to the size of the transfers, substantial. There are good reasons to expect such effects. Poverty is associated with stress, depression and anxiety, and experimental evidence suggests that the relationship is at least partly causal [23]; financial scarcity also imposes a cognitive burden that may impair decision-making [24]. Historical evidence from the Canadian Mincome experiment found reductions in hospitalisation, particularly for mental-health diagnoses [13], and quasi-experimental evidence from casino dividends paid to Cherokee families shows improvements in children's behavioural and emotional outcomes [14]. A scoping review of interventions similar to basic income concluded that they improve some health outcomes, particularly mental health, although evidence is limited [27].",
