        "Nine pilots report food security. Food insecurity falls in every one, and the pooled reduction is 5.6 percentage points (95 percent confidence interval 3.9 to 7.3), from a control-group prevalence of 34 percent. Expressed in standard-deviation units, the effect is 0.23, larger than the effect on wellbeing. The largest reductions are found in Magnolia, Tacoma and Kenya, where baseline food insecurity was highest. Consumption data, available for six pilots, show that recipients spent most of the additional money on food, utilities, housing costs and transport, with no evidence of increased spending on alcohol or tobacco, consistent with the broader cash-transfer literature [7][9].",
