        "The pilots were designed to measure the effects of receiving a transfer, not the effects of a society-wide programme financed by taxation. Scaling up raises three issues that the pilots cannot address directly: general-equilibrium effects on prices and wages, the behavioural and distributional effects of the taxes needed to finance a universal scheme, and the interaction with existing social insurance and assistance [1][26]. Evidence from Kenya suggests that local multipliers from cash transfers can be large and price effects small in settings with slack resources [8], but this need not generalise to economies with full employment and tight housing markets. Evidence from Alaska suggests that permanent universal transfers can raise demand for local labour, offsetting any reduction in individual labour supply [10].",
        "Table 6 illustrates the fiscal arithmetic for Korea. We consider four schemes and calculate their gross cost, the savings from consolidating overlapping cash benefits, the revenue lost through labour supply responses implied by our pooled estimate and the resulting net cost, all as shares of 2022 GDP of about KRW 2,160 trillion. Extending the Gyeonggi Youth Dividend nationally, at KRW 1 million for every 24-year-old, would cost about 0.03 percent of GDP — trivial in fiscal terms, but also small as a transfer. A universal payment of KRW 100,000 a month to all residents would cost 2.9 percent of GDP gross. A payment of KRW 500,000 a month to every adult, close to the generosity of the low-income pilots in relation to local incomes, would cost 12.2 percent of GDP, roughly half of current general government revenue.",
