        "Our review highlights several gaps. First, almost all pilots are short and explicitly temporary. Recipients know that payments will end, which may dampen labour supply responses because people are reluctant to give up jobs they will need again, and may also limit investment responses. The Kenyan pilot and, in a different way, the Alaska dividend are the only sources of evidence on long-horizon or permanent transfers [2][10]. Second, the pilots are partial: they treat individuals or small groups within larger economies, so they cannot capture changes in wages, prices, social norms or employer behaviour that a universal programme might bring about [3][8].",
